            // A backstop, not a supply (2026-09-27): the meter counted its 2 kW for 24 hours, 48
            // kWh a day, as though the home made it. Now the meter shows it apart, as what it CAN
            // add while fuel is burned for it. The machine: a Honda EU2200i class inverter
            // generator. Its owner's manual (https://cdn.powerequipment.honda.com/pe/pdf/manuals/
            // 00X31Z446010.pdf, read 2026-09-27): "Rated output" "1.8 kVA", "Maximum output"
            // "2.2 kVA", "Fuel tank capacity 0.95 US gal (3.6 L)"; a Honda dealer's listing
            // (https://hondapowertools.com/product/eu2200i-generator/, read 2026-09-27): "3.2hr @
            // rated load", "8.1 hrs @ 1/4 load". So 1,800 W while it runs, on 3.6 L / 3.2 h =
            // 1.125 L an hour at that load, 0.63 L a kWh (at a quarter load it is 0.44 L an hour,
            // about 1.0 L a kWh, which is why a backstop charges the batteries flat out). It was
            // 2,000 W on 1.5 L an hour from no source. It burns what is in its 200 L drum.
