        // THE HOUSEHOLD'S WATER (2026-09-27). Three people at data/home_outline.json's one-person
        // fixture list (drinking and cooking 5, kitchen sink 15, bathroom sink 5, shower 35,
        // toilet 14, laundry 11 L a day; home_solo.ron carries the same list for one), 255 L a
        // day. Until this the household tap drew 245 L a day on top of fixtures whose ports drew
        // about 1,270 more, and the hot ports added to 403 L a day. Each fixture's hot share is
        // the measured share of that fixture's water that is hot, from the Water Research
        // Foundation's Residential End Uses of Water, Version 2 (DeOreo et al. 2016, 94 homes in
        // Arizona, Colorado, Florida, Georgia, Texas, Washington and Ontario), as tabled by Lawrence Berkeley
        // National Laboratory for the EPA's WaterSense programme (Chen et al., "Calculating
        // Average Hot Water Mixes of Residential Plumbing Fittings", June 2020, Table 2,
        // https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1619186, read 2026-09-27): "Showers ... 66.2",
        // "Faucets ... 57.0", "Clothes washers ... 20.0" percent. So the kitchen sink 45 L (25.7
        // hot), the bathroom sink 15 (8.6 hot), the shower 105 (69.5 hot) and the washer 33
        // (6.6 hot): 110.3 L of hot water a day, 43% of the household's water. Two checks: the
        // same table's ten Seattle homes (DeOreo and Mayer 2000) found "an overall hot water
        // usage of 39.6 percent of total indoor water use", and the Energy Saving Trust's
        // measured UK homes ("Measurement of Domestic Hot Water Consumption in Dwellings", 2008,
        // https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a75a29ced915d6faf2b4829/3147-measure-domestic-hot-water-consump.pdf,
        // read 2026-09-27) fit "40 + 28 N" litres a day for N people, 124 for three. The water
        // heater makes exactly the 110.3 L the fixtures draw.
