        "wind_turbine_small": (
            shape: "cylinder",
            size: (0.1, 2.4, 0.1),
            color: (0.85, 0.86, 0.9),
            label: "Wind turbine",
            category: "Power",
            stats: [
                (
                    kind: "power",
                    value: "+0.11 kWh/d here: the wind averages 2.4 m/s, under the 3.1 it starts at",
                    status: "warn",
                ),
            ],
            // What the turbine makes at the home's site (2026-09-27; the meter counted its 150 W
            // for 24 hours, 3.6 kWh a day, and the Energy loop +0.7 at an unsourced 19% capacity
            // factor). The machine: Primus Wind Power's AIR 40, whose owner's manual (page 5,
            // https://www.manualslib.com/manual/2040471/Primus-Wind-Power-Air-40.html, read
            // 2026-09-27) gives "Rated Power: 160 watts @ 28 mph (12.5 m/s)", "Start Up Wind
            // Speed: 7 mph (3.13 m/s)", "Rotor Diameter: 46 in / 1.17 m" and "Kilowatt
            // Hours/month: 40 kWh/month @ 12 mph (5.5 m/s)". The site's wind: the Western
            // Regional Climate Center's "Average Wind Speeds - MPH", "based on the hourly data
            // from 1996-2006 from automated stations at reporting airports"
            // (https://wrcc-archive.dri.edu/Climate/comp_table_show.php?stype=wind_speed_avg,
            // read 2026-09-27), "BREMERTON AIRPORT AWOS |KPWT|1996-2006|" annual "5.3" mph, 2.37
            // m/s (August 4.6, March 6.3), the nearest station, about 18 km from Silverdale; the
            // turbine is taken at the station's wind-sensor height (10 m, the standard). The
            // method is the one small turbines are rated by: the AWEA Small Wind Turbine
            // Performance and Safety Standard 9.1-2009's Rated Annual Energy is computed
            // "assuming a Rayleigh wind speed distribution"
            // (https://cleanpower.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/AWEA_2009_Small_Turbine_Standard.pdf,
            // read 2026-09-27). The turbine's power taken as a cube law from its 3.13 m/s start,
            // capped at 160 W, and fitted to the manual's own 40 kWh a month at 5.5 m/s (that
            // implies a power coefficient of 0.36, generous for a 1.17 m rotor), a Rayleigh wind
            // averaging 2.37 m/s gives 4.4 W: 0.11 kWh a day, a 2.8% capacity factor. The same
            // curve on the twelve monthly means gives 4.7 W, and a power coefficient of 0.30 gives
            // 3.7: range 3.7 to 4.7, point 4.4 (src/machines.rs recomputes it). For scale, PNNL's
            // Distributed Wind Market Report 2024 edition: "The average net capacity factor in 2023
            // for a sample of 100 small wind projects was 13%" (read 2026-09-27), turbines sited
            // where the wind is. `watts` is what the electrical sim makes around the clock (it has
            // no wind model) and what the meter counts, so it is the day's average; the port keeps
            // the 160 W nameplate, which sizes the cable.
            power: Some(Generator(
                watts: 4.4,
                fuel_lph: 0.0,
            )),
            ports: [
                (
                    utility: Electricity,
                    dir: Out,
                    label: "160 W at 12.5 m/s",
                    watts: 160.0,
                    flow_lpm: 0.0,
                    mbps: 0.0,
                    anchor: (0.0, 0.0, 0.0),
                ),
            ],
            storage: [],
            auto_recipe: None,
            container_type: None,
            model: None,
        ),
