    loops: [
        (
            name: "Energy",
            demand: "~61.8 kWh/day in the Realistic mode (the home and its machines ~24.0, the air handlers ~37.8); ~24.0 kWh/day Station-supplied (water heater ~9.5, tower pumps ~4.1, mushroom tent humidifiers ~3.4, freezer ~1.5, the mushrooms' CO2 fans ~1.5, the fish tanks' air pumps ~1.2, the rest ~2.6); cooking and crafting on top, by the hour",
            supply: "~11.0 kWh/day: 10 solar panels ~10.9 (their yield at the home's site after the batteries' round trip; ~5.1 in January) + wind ~0.1; 8 batteries; the backstop generator (1.8 kW while it runs) counted apart",
            closes: false,
            weakest: true,
            note: "Does NOT close in either mode, on the year's average or in winter. THE METER (2026-09-27): these figures are the Construction page's power meter, which charges every machine its real daily energy and credits every source what it makes at the home's site (Silverdale, WA, data/self_sufficiency/location.ron), and a test (the_household_machines_charge_their_sourced_daily_energy in src/machines.rs) holds this loop to it. SUPPLY: a 400 W panel makes 1.09 kWh a day there averaged over the year and 0.51 in January, the worst month (NREL's PVWatts: 440.7 kWh a year at the inverter for a panel tilted 40 degrees to the south, less the batteries' 90% round trip, Tesla Powerwall 2), and the wind turbine (a Primus AIR 40, 160 W at 12.5 m/s) makes 0.11 kWh a day, because the nearest measured wind, the Bremerton airport's, averages 2.4 m/s (5.3 mph) and the turbine needs 3.1 to start. So the home makes about 11.0 kWh a day on its own, about 5.2 in January. The backstop generator (a 1.8 kW inverter set, 1.1 L of fuel an hour) is not counted as supply: the meter shows what it can add while it runs. Until this the meter counted the generator's 2 kW and the turbine's 150 W for 24 hours and each panel's 400 W for 4.5 sun-hours with nothing lost, 69.6 kWh a day and 'fully self-sufficient', and this loop's 15.1 rested on a generic 4.5 sun-hours and an unsourced 0.7 kWh a day of wind. DEMAND: the household's machines are EIA's 2020 Residential Energy Consumption Survey averages for a three-person household (Tables CE5.3a and CE5.3b): the water heater is the home's largest load, 9.5 kWh a day (3,482 kWh a year for a home that heats its water with electricity, standby losses and the laundry's hot water included); the freezer 1.5 (559 kWh a year); the washer's motor 0.21 (77 kWh a year). The towers' pumps are 4.1 (Tower Garden's 48 W and 35 W pumps on the maker's indoor timer, 5 minutes on in every 50), and the three fish tanks' air pumps 1.2 (a 17 W HIBLOW HP-20 each, all day, the top of FAO's 5 to 8 L of air a minute for each cubic metre of water). In the Station-supplied mode, the default, the station's own plant powers the air handlers and the scrubber, and the home draws about 24.0 kWh a day against the 11.0 it makes: the water heater 9.5, the tower pumps 4.1, the mushroom tent humidifiers 3.4, the freezer 1.5, the mushrooms' CO2 fans 1.5 (the room's 1.2, the tents' 0.3), the fish tanks' air pumps 1.2 and the rest 2.6 (the home server, the defense turret, the water purifier and pump, the network, the irrigation, the washer, and the work stations at idle). It does NOT close: 13.0 kWh a day short, about 12 more panels on the year's average and about 37 more for January; the backstop would have to run about 7.2 hours a day on 8.1 L of fuel to cover it. Cooking and crafting come on top, by the hour: the work stations draw 3.6 kW more while a craft runs at all of them, and a real three-person household's cooking averages 0.89 kWh a day (RECS Table CE5.3b, 325 kWh a year). Not counted yet, because no data carries them: lighting, small appliances, and the fish tanks' water pumps (FAO sizes 25 to 50 W for a 1,000 L unit; how these tanks' water reaches the towers is not modelled). In the Realistic mode the air handlers' 37.8 kWh a day come onto the home's grid as well, about 61.8 kWh a day against 11.0: it does NOT close, about 47 more panels on the year's average, and even the backstop running all day (43.2 kWh on 27 L of fuel) would leave it 7.6 kWh short. The cheapest lever in the house is the water heater (a heat pump water heater moves heat instead of making it; not placed or sourced here yet), and its RECS figure is about twice the heat in this home's own 110 L of hot water a day (the Water loop): the average US household it comes from draws more. Aboard a sealed station every litre the crops breathe out has to be condensed back out of the air, because there is no outside to vent it to; until this the model held the home's air at 40% for nothing. The home now carries seven air handlers (Carrier 42CT size 14 fan coils on the station's cooling loop: 1,842 m3/h, 325 W flat out, data/life_support.ron): two in the greenhouse and one in the court, holding them at 75% (the greenhouse's crops breathe out about 541 L a day, the court's towers 106 L), and four in the plant room, holding the home's own air at 50%. Measured over a day and a night at full planting (src/systems/farming/life_support_tests.rs) they average about 1,570 W, 37.8 kWh a day. Most of it is the home's four: the greenhouse and the court leak 0.5 air changes an hour into the drier home air, about 215 L of their water a day, and every litre condensed there costs about 105 Wh of fan power against 27 Wh in the greenhouse. The exhaust fan now idles (the handlers hold the greenhouse under its 80%), and the CO2 scrubber that replaced the 25 W air recycler idles too (the crops keep the carbon dioxide far under its 2,636 ppm). Since 2026-09-27 each mushroom tent has a CO2 fan (an AC Infinity CLOUDLINE S4, 28 W, switched at 900 ppm by a CO2 controller) and the mushroom room one of its own (a CLOUDLINE S6, 70 W, at 600 ppm): about 61 W together (the room fan 51, on about 72% of the time), 1.5 kWh a day, and the fresh air they bring in makes the tent humidifiers work harder, 3.4 kWh a day at the average the catalog carries (was 0.6). The cheaper levers are design calls: holding the home's air at 60% instead of 50% (the EPA's line; the handlers then average about 920 W, 22 kWh a day, measured), sealing the greenhouse and the court from the home more tightly than an Earth greenhouse's 0.5 air changes an hour, or fan coils with EC motors (the catalogue's are permanent split capacitor motors, the least efficient kind, and its EC option publishes no figures to model). The coils' heat, about 19 kW of it latent (666 L a day at 2.45 MJ a litre, FAO-56), goes to the station's radiators, which the game does not model yet: the Climate loop. Grow-lights to feed 3 people indoors would draw 30 to 150+ kWh/d, so the garden uses the sun, not LEDs.",
        ),
        (
            name: "Water",
            demand: "~346 L/day net (household ~255, 110 of it hot + the garden's net ~91)",
            supply: "rain + well + ~155 L/day greywater recycling; the air handlers hand ~689 L/day back to the tanks; 8000 L cistern = ~24 days",
            closes: true,
            weakest: false,
            note: "Closes, and the garden's water is now a real loop (2026-09-26, ship life support). The irrigation draws what the crops breathe out plus what their tissue keeps (NASA's measured share, 2 to 10% by crop, data/life_support.ron), about 780 L a day at full planting, and the seven air handlers condense what the air carries and pipe it back to the cistern: about 689 L a day, every litre the indoor crops (647), the mushroom tents (7 breathed, 29 humidified, up from 6 since their CO2 fans bring in drier air) and the household (5) put into the air, to within 1% (src/systems/farming/life_support_tests.rs). The garden's net draw is therefore about 91 L a day: the water its crops keep (~40 L, which leaves in the harvest), what the outdoor fields breathe into the weather (~57 L), less the household's breath the coils catch (~5 L). Net demand is the household plus that, about 346 L a day, and the cistern's dry-season reach is about 23 days (it was quoted at 32 when the garden's water was billed at a fraction of its real rate and its vapour vanished into the home's air, and at 24 against a 240 L household). THE HOUSEHOLD (2026-09-27): three people at data/home_outline.json's one-person fixture list, 255 L a day (drinking and cooking 15, the kitchen sink 45, the bathroom sink 15, the shower 105, the toilet 42, the laundry 33); until then a 240 L household tap drew on top of fixtures whose ports drew about 1,270 L a day more, and whose hot ports added to 403 L a day against the heater's 432. Each fixture's hot water is its measured share (the Water Research Foundation's Residential End Uses of Water, 94 homes: showers 66.2%, faucets 57.0%, clothes washers 20.0%), 110 L a day, 43% of the household's water, and the water heater makes exactly that; the washer now has the hot and cold hoses a real one has (data/machines/home.ron quotes the sources). The air runs on game hours and the tanks on real minutes, which is still the operator's open question; the flows cross as litres a day on both sides, so a day's water balances on either clock. If the air handlers lose power, the greenhouse's water stays in the air and the tanks stop getting it back.",
        ),
        (
            name: "Air",
            demand: "~5.9 kg/day of carbon dioxide breathed out (three people ~2.3, the mushroom tents ~3.6) and ~4.5 kg/day of oxygen breathed in",
            supply: "the crops' photosynthesis (they take up the carbon dioxide and give out ~0.72 kg of oxygen per kg of it); CO2 scrubber (ISS CDRA, 860 W) above 2,636 ppm; seven air handlers for the water; a CO2 fan in each mushroom tent and one for the mushroom room",
            closes: false,
            weakest: false,
            note: "The carbon closes on the crops; the oxygen does not quite (2026-09-26, ship life support, data/life_support.ron). People breathe in proportion to the food they burn (NASA's crew figures, 0.835 kg of oxygen in and 0.998 kg of carbon dioxide out at 11.82 MJ, scaled to the Food loop's 6,600 kcal: 1.95 and 2.33 kg), and the tents' substrate breathes out 1.09 g of carbon dioxide per kg an hour. The crops take it up through the pores they breathe water out of, at NASA's measured grams per litre, in their lit hours, and less in thin air (Kimball: 33% more per doubling). Over a measured day and night at full planting they took up about 5.0 of the 5.9 kg while the home's air went through 440 to 510 ppm; they catch up as it rises, and it settles near 675 ppm (measured: started at 700, its day ran 597 to 689 with the crops taking up 5.77 kg; started at 800, 651 to 751 and 5.99), so the scrubber idles. Oxygen falls short: the crops give out one molecule of oxygen for each of carbon dioxide they fix, but people burning fat and protein take in more oxygen than the carbon dioxide they breathe out (respiratory quotient 0.87), so once the carbon settles the home still loses about 0.3 kg of oxygen a day, plus about 0.1 kg in the 0.46 kg of air a day that leaks overboard (BVAD's 0.02 kg a day per module, each of the home's 23 rooms counted as one). That is years before it matters out of the ~2,900 kg in the home's air, but it is not closed: an electrolyser splitting water would make it back for about 6.5 kWh per kg of oxygen (data/home_outline.json), about 2.6 kWh a day, and the home does not carry one yet. The leaked nitrogen has to be brought in. The mushrooms' own air is held since 2026-09-27: each tent's CO2 fan (an AC Infinity CLOUDLINE S4 on a CO2 controller) holds it at 900 ppm, under the 1,000 the oysters fruit under, and the room's (a CLOUDLINE S6) holds the room near 600, so every tent sits at 900 ppm and 90% humidity where they ran 1,570 to 1,640 ppm; stale air now costs a mushroom its crop (26.7% per 1,000 ppm over, data/garden/humidity.ron). That holds on a game's first days. Once the home's air settles near 675 ppm it does not: the room sits at 690 to 780 ppm with its fan flat out, and the tents' fans hold 900 only by pulling so much of that air through that their humidifiers, flat out, let the tents fall to 71 to 82% humidity, under the oyster's 85% (measured). The mushrooms make 3.6 of the home's 5.9 kg of carbon dioxide a day and cannot fruit in the air they enrich: a home of their own for their air, or a way for it to reach the crops, is the next rung. The carbon closes in either Ship life support mode; the mode only decides who powers the machines.",
        ),
        (
            name: "Food",
            demand: "~6,600 kcal/day + complete nutrition (3 people)",
            supply: "indoor sun-lit garden (variety + herb towers, potato/grain/oilseed beds, mushroom racks, aquaponic fish tanks): ALL veg/herbs/protein/vitamins + B12 + omega-3; outdoor grain, legume, tuber and oilseed fields add bulk calories and fat; silo = ~750 days. The calories are the grown line, computed from the crops placed",
            closes: false,
            weakest: true,
            note: "Does not close (2026-09-27): the placed crops grow about 6,020 kcal a day against the 6,600 three people need, 91%, and that is an upper bound. It closed at about 7,350 until two defects were fixed. The seven mushroom racks counted an unsourced placeholder of about 198 kcal a day each; a rack is ten 5 lb fruiting blocks, and a block gives about 1 lb of oyster mushrooms over four flushes and 53 days on its shelf, so a rack grows about 28 (data/garden/yields.ron, MUSHROOMS). And sunflower and paddy rice counted their hulls as food, so the oilseed beds and field lost a quarter and the rice trays a fifth. About ten more potato beds (61.5 kcal a day each) would close it on paper; the fish tanks are not counted. The garden is now a real indoor farm, but honestly area-capped. Sun-lit (skylight) single canopy: the variety and herb towers cover ALL vegetables, herbs, plant protein, and plant vitamins for 3. Potato/grain/oilseed beds, dark mushroom racks, and a small aquaponic fish loop add real calories, fats, and the B12 + omega-3 no plant can make; the outdoor field crops add bulk grain, pulses and oil; the silo buffers winter. The grown figure is computed from the crops placed (2026-09-26): each plant at its cited yield, cropped back to back all year, so it is an upper bound that does not yet discount for the light the canopy really gets, and the fish tanks' figure is an estimate the crop model cannot count. The hard limit is light: free sunlight is ONE canopy, and a real sun-lit greenhouse here gives about 7.2 kcal/m2/day across the year (data/home_outline.json). Grow-lights to feed 3 indoors would need 2.5x to 12x the whole energy budget, so we do not, and that is the lesson.",
            food_demand_kcal: Some(6600.0),
        ),
        (
